Spec: ServiceRestoreScript

Platform: Rhai script library (vynil-core 0.7.7 engine)

Purpose:
  Orchestrate the immediate (no schedule branch exists here) restic restore of one service
    package inside a dispatched restore Pod: decode the Pod's own process env into the args map
    via @ctx::from_args, then walk a fixed @import_run stage sequence — pre-hook, the
    maintenance-start, the restic restore itself, the optional prepare hook, the three engine
    legs in secret → postgresql → mysql order, maintenance-stop, post-hook — where one package
    script can replace the whole lane and each engine leg can be individually replaced by its
    own package script. No CR is ever in hand: the instance is invisible at this level (frozen
    by /agent/src/service/restore.sdd), no status/condition/tag is written here, and the
    failure surface is exactly five @throw strings over custom-script return codes plus
    whatever the stages re-throw. Shape-twin of ./../tenant/restore.rhai (diff-verified: the
    twin adds two more engine legs, mongodb and redis, before maintenance_stop and is
    otherwise byte-for-byte this flow) and stage-shape sibling of ./backup.sdd.

Exposes:
  @run(@args) — the module's sole @fn and entry point; invoked as `restore::run(args)` by the
    Rust lane /agent/src/service/restore.sdd — the only module name its fixed snippet names is
    `restore` — whose `let _ =` discards the returned @Dynamic.

Accepts:
  @args — the ten-key CLI `args` map injected by the Rust lane. Read directly by this file:
    `package_dir` (all five custom-script @is_file gates, spelled `${args.package_dir}`) plus,
    through the merged map, `s3_url` and `package_dir` (restore.sh command interpolation).
    @ctx::from_args mutates this SAME map in place and returns it as `context`, so the
    `args.package_dir` / `context.package_dir` spellings name one value — the 19-key env
    contract and `package_dir`'s untouched pass-through are owned by ./../lib/backup_context.sdd.
  @context downstream consumers (this file never reads the lists — it only says which stage
    runs): `snapshot` + `s3_url` + `restic_args` (consumed by ./restore_run.rhai — the
    WHAT-restores decision, env `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` copied verbatim), `secret_list` +
    `namespace` + the CLI-passed-through `template_dir` (./restore_secret.rhai), `pg_list`
    (./restore_postgresql.rhai), `mysql_list` (./restore_mysql.rhai),
    `deployment_list` / `statefulset_list` / `scale_target` (the maintenance pair's unwritten
    scale TO-DO only — read by no executed code today), while `restic_tags`, the four
    `max_*` retention keys, `sub_path`, `base_path`, `mongo_list` and `redis_list` have no
    reader in the service restore lane as wired.
  Runtime inputs — five optional package scripts under `${args.package_dir}/scripts/`:
    `restore.sh` (replaces the whole lane), `restore_prepare.sh` (an additive hook with NO
    shipped module and NO engine-bypass), `restore_secret.sh`, `restore_postgresql.sh`,
    `restore_mysql.sh` (per-leg replacements). Each gate is pure file existence; each executed
    script is a shell entry, not a Rhai module.

Returns:
  Custom-script path: @run hits `return rc` after `restore.sh` succeeds — `rc` there is
    necessarily 0 (a non-zero one threw first) — and the Rust lane discards it.
  Normal path: the implicit value is the trailing `import_run("restore_post", ...)` result
    (unit on a stock image, since `restore_post` ships nowhere); the Rust lane discards this
    too (@common/src/rhaihandler.sdd skip semantics).
  The observable result is entirely side-effect: a restic restore landing on `/` inside the
    Pod, Secrets re-applied through the cluster bridge, psql/mysql re-seeds from SQL dumps
    under `/backup`, and the 0-or-throw completion channel the Rust exit-5 lane reads.

Raises:
  Exactly five @throw statements, all the same shape — template text
    `${context.package_dir}/scripts/<name> FAILED returning ${rc}` — one per custom script
    (`restore.sh`, `restore_prepare.sh`, `restore_secret.sh`, `restore_postgresql.sh`,
    `restore_mysql.sh`) on a non-zero @shell_run code. Throws from the restore.sh gate onward
    abort mid-lane; the thrown text reaches the process through the Rust lane's
    `Rhai script error:` prefix and exit-5 (frozen by /agent/src/service/restore.sdd —
    contrast backup's exit-4 — no condition, no `set_status_*` marker is minted at this level
    or by any shipped service stage).
  Stage re-throws propagate identically: @import_run swallows only the two skip markers
    (`No ${name} module, skipping.` / `No ${name}::run function, skipping.`; owners
    /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd + the vynil-core shim), so a `restic backup FAILED returning ${rc}`
    from ./restore_run.rhai (the verbatim misnomer: the RESTORE stage throws "backup"), a
    `psql failed for ${pg}` from ./restore_postgresql.rhai, a `mysql restoration failed for ${mysql}`
    from ./restore_mysql.rhai or a cluster-bridge raise from ./restore_secret.rhai aborts the run.
  Import-time: the module body's sole top-level statement `import "backup_context" as ctx;`
    resolves at module load; a resolution miss is a Rust-lane eval error before @run starts.
    @ctx::from_args itself raises for nothing env-side (./../lib/backup_context.sdd), and
    `context.s3_url` / `context.package_dir` are unguarded reads both always present (the
    former possibly the degraded `s3:/`, which the lane neither detects nor reports — it rides
    the restore.sh export and restic's own return codes downstream).

Handles:
  Total-lane custom restore — `restore.sh` present: run it with the repo export, then
    `import_run("restore_post")` and return — skipping the maintenance pair, restore_run, the
    prepare hook and every engine leg. Absent (the stock case) falls through to the full
    sequence; `restore_pre` still ran before this gate either way.
  Snapshot choice — the env `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` is the sole WHAT-decider: @ctx::from_args copies
    it verbatim into `snapshot` and ./restore_run.rhai interpolates it straight into
    `restic restore ${context.snapshot} --exclude-xattr security.selinux --target /`; no
    latest-resolution, listing or filtering happens at this level, and an empty env still
    ships an empty snapshot token on the command line, leaving resolution and failure to
    restic's return code. The destination likewise arrives only as `BASE_REPO_URL` +
    `SUB_PATH` normalized into `s3_url`.
  Engine routing is file-existence ONLY, never list contents: each of secret/postgresql/mysql
    takes its own script-or-module branch independently and in the fixed secret → postgresql →
    mysql order. The asymmetry against ./backup.rhai: the `restore_prepare.sh` gate has no
    else-branch — passing it adds its run but never bypasses the three legs (backup's twin
    gate DOES replace the block) — and no `restore_prepare` module ships to be shadowed.
    Conversely ./restore_run.rhai has no `.sh` replacement gate, and this lane has no
    backup_init / backup_check / backup_prune counterpart at all (nothing to init or verify:
    the repository already exists at restore time).
  Maintenance window positions: @import_run("maintenance_start") sits right before
    restore_run and @import_run("maintenance_stop") after the engine ladder — the window
    brackets the whole restore plus every engine re-seed. Both shipped modules are
    pass-through hook calls around a scale TO-DO comment (deployment_list/statefulset_list
    to 0 / back to `scale_target`): the window currently toggles nothing on the cluster, and
    this file itself never touches scale anywhere — `SCALE_TARGET` decodes into `scale_target`
    with no executed reader on either side of the TODO.
  Unshipped hook names: `restore_pre` and `restore_post` ship in no engine directory
    (./, ./../tenant, ./../lib) — on a stock image both take the debug-log skip; they exist
    as package `scripts/` slots.
  RESTIC environment handling — this file exports ONLY on the restore.sh line, the inline
    `export RESTIC_REPOSITORY="${context.s3_url}";` prefix; the other four custom scripts run
    bare `${context.package_dir}/scripts/<name>` with no export. TLS rides `restic_args`
    (`" --insecure-tls"`, appended by ./restore_run.rhai) and the Pod env; `RESTIC_HOST` and
    `RESTIC_TAGS` are never read or written here.
  No cron/schedule branch — scheduling lives exclusively in ./install.sdd's backup lanes;
    this module, reached by `agent service restore` inside a dispatched restore Job or any
    process-env already shaped like one, always restores now.

Must:
  Execute exactly this order with nothing else interleaved: @ctx::from_args(args) →
    @import_run("restore_pre") → `restore.sh` gate (run + `restore_post` + `return` on presence,
    OR fall through) → @import_run("maintenance_start") → @import_run("restore_run") →
    `restore_prepare.sh` gate (run or skip; flow always continues) → `restore_secret.sh` gate
    (run, OR import the module) → `restore_postgresql.sh` gate (run, OR import) →
    `restore_mysql.sh` gate (run, OR import) → @import_run("maintenance_stop") →
    @import_run("restore_post") → end.
  Emit no log statement at this level (contrast ./backup.rhai's single
    `Starting backup using target:` line) — every log line of a stock restore comes from a
    stage module.
  Ship the restore.sh command with the exact prefix `export RESTIC_REPOSITORY="${context.s3_url}";`
    followed by `${context.package_dir}/scripts/restore.sh`, and ship the other four custom
    scripts as the bare `${context.package_dir}/scripts/<name>` command with no export.
  Treat any non-zero @shell_run code as fatal at its own throw site (never retried, never
    collected; `restore.sh` rc 0 alone returning).
  Pass the one `context` map to every @import_run and discard every return — the
    `context = import_run(...)` merge discipline of ./install.sdd is absent here, so stages
    communicate through their own side effects (restic, `/backup`, the cluster bridge, shell
    env), never through a returned map.
  Resolve every stage name against the four resolver directories frozen by the Rust lane
    ({package_dir}/scripts first), so a package `scripts/restore_run.rhai` (or any other stage
    name) replaces the shipped lane wholesale.
  Keep the gate presence check spelled `${args.package_dir}` while the command and throw text
    spell `${context.package_dir}` — two spellings, one value through the in-place merge.
  Keep the import-time surface at the single `import "backup_context" as ctx;` —
    ./../lib/backup_context.rhai executes nothing on load, so module load touches no env,
    filesystem or cluster.

Must not:
  Write any failure/success marker — no `set_status_*` call, no condition, no tag, no
    `instance` binding, no kube call: the Rust lane froze this side as CR-blind and any status
    write here would fabricate an object the restore Job never owns.
  Branch on env to pick scheduled-vs-immediate or to skip a stage wholesale: the only env
    influence on this lane is the DECODED env inside @from_args (destination, snapshot, engine
    lists, TLS), never a schedule or feature switch.
  Read `context.snapshot`, the three engine lists or `mongo_list` / `redis_list` to route
    work — routing here is @is_file only, the lists are consumed by the stages, and mongodb
    and redis have no leg at all in this service lane.

Depends on:
  ./../lib/backup_context.rhai — the single import; @from_args half of the 19-key env→context
    contract (spec ./../lib/backup_context.sdd, which freezes from_args and this file as its
    consumer).
  The shipped stage modules of this directory, all specs pending and each self-throwing:
    ./maintenance_start.rhai, ./restore_run.rhai (restic restore ${context.snapshot}
    --exclude-xattr security.selinux --target / ${context.restic_args}), ./restore_secret.rhai
    (Secret apply from `/backup/${secret}` files, target name from the
    `secret_${name}_target` env, hbs partials from `context.template_dir`),
    ./restore_postgresql.rhai (psql from `/backup/postgresql_${pg}[_cleaned].sql`),
    ./restore_mysql.rhai (mysql from `/backup/mysql_${mysql}[_cleaned].sql`),
    ./maintenance_stop.rhai.
  vynil-core 0.7.7 engine API — the @import_run shim with its two skip markers, @is_file and
    @shell_run (int return code); registrars per /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd. This level
    calls no @log_info and no cluster function.

References:
  /agent/src/service/restore.sdd — the caller: the fixed `restore`-only snippet, ten-key
    `args`, the resolver directories and the exit-5 lane every throw here lands in.
  ./backup.sdd — the same-kind sibling spec: the stage-shape twin with a block-replacing
    prepare gate, the export prefix on every custom script, the repo init/check pair and its
    one log line.
  ./../tenant/restore.rhai — the diff-verified twin (mongodb + redis legs added before
    maintenance_stop; unspecified).
  /agent/tests/rhai_backup_redis.rs — the nearest test file (Tasks; mock engine points at
    scripts/lib + scripts/tenant, tenant-flavored).

Scenario: custom restore.sh replaces the whole lane
  Given `${args.package_dir}/scripts/restore.sh` exists in the mounted package scripts
  When @run executes and the script exits 0
  Then the RESTIC_REPOSITORY-exported copy ran, @import_run("restore_post") followed and @run
    returned the rc — maintenance_start, restore_run, the prepare hook, every engine leg and
    maintenance_stop never ran
  But given the script exits 3
  Then the throw text is `${context.package_dir}/scripts/restore.sh FAILED returning 3` and no
    restore_post import runs

Scenario: snapshot env decides the restored snapshot verbatim
  Given process env `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` set to `abcd1234` with the repo envs present
  When the stock flow reaches ./restore_run.rhai
  Then `restic restore abcd1234 --exclude-xattr security.selinux --target /` runs with the
    merged `restic_args` — this file never inspects, validates or latest-resolves the token
  But given `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` is unset
  Then `snapshot` is `""`, the empty token still rides the command line, and restic's own
    non-zero rc surfaces as the misnamed stage throw — no fail-fast at either level

Scenario: engine ladder order and per-leg replacement
  Given no `restore_prepare.sh` and no per-leg scripts
  When @run passes the restore_run step
  Then @import_run fires secret → postgresql → mysql in that fixed order on the one context
    map, whatever the three lists hold (empty lists still import_run, the loops no-op)
  Given the same package ships `restore_postgresql.sh` only
  Then the postgresql leg is the exported-free shell run and the other two legs stay module
    imports

Scenario: restore_prepare.sh is additive, not a block replacement
  Given `restore_prepare.sh` exists and exits 0
  Then the three engine legs still run afterwards — the gate has no else-branch and no
    `restore_prepare` module exists for it to shadow
  But given it exits 5
  Then the throw names `restore_prepare.sh FAILED returning 5` before any engine leg runs

Scenario: stock image skips unshipped hooks and returns via the post-hook
  Given a package with no custom scripts and a stock engine directory set
  When @run completes the normal path
  Then `restore_pre` and `restore_post` hit the two debug-log skip markers (they ship nowhere)
    and @run returns that unit
  And this module emitted no log of its own — every `Starting ... restore` line in the output
    belongs to ./restore_run.rhai

Scenario: stage throw aborts mid-window
  Given the sequence reached ./restore_run.rhai and restic restore exits 2
  Then the stage's own throw propagates through @import_run: the prepare gate, every engine
    leg, maintenance_stop and the restore_post hook never run and the Rust lane exits 5 behind
    `Rhai script error: `

Tasks:
  [ ] Map the six Scenarios to tests: /agent/tests/rhai_backup_redis.rs runs a mock engine
      over scripts/lib + scripts/tenant and names only `restore_redis`/`backup_prepare_redis`
      — zero coverage of this orchestrator or of any service restore stage (rhai_lib.rs is
      lib-only and calls no restore fn).
  [?] The restore stage misnomers its own failure: ./restore_run.rhai throws
      `restic backup FAILED returning ${rc}` for a RESTORE — correct the text or pin it with
      a test before anything downstream routes on it.
  [?] `restore_prepare.sh` has no else-branch and no module: decide whether the prepare gate
      should replace the engine block (./backup.rhai symmetry) or is deliberately an additive
      hook the ladder runs after.
  [?] Export asymmetry: only the restore.sh command carries the RESTIC_REPOSITORY export; the
      other four custom scripts run without it (backup exports all six) — confirm the package
      restore scripts are expected to source the repo env themselves.
  [?] The maintenance window toggles nothing: ./maintenance_start.rhai and
      ./maintenance_stop.rhai contain only hook passthroughs around a scale TO-DO, so a
      full-filesystem restore and DB re-seeds run against live workloads — decide whether to
      implement the deployment/statefulset scaling (down to 0, up to `scale_target`) or
      delete the window.
  [?] restore_pre/restore_post ship in no engine directory — package-side hook slots only,
      same posture as ./install.sdd's unshipped hooks; confirm intentional (the Rust-lane
      spec carries the matching open point).
  [?] The restore.sh replacement also skips maintenance_stop and the rc-0 return is discarded
      by Rust — confirm the package-owned-restore semantics (failure then requires the
      package script to exit non-zero, and any future scaled state stays scaled).
  [?] Service-vs-tenant leg gap: @ctx::from_args always decodes `mongo_list` and
      `redis_list` but this lane has no restore leg for them (the tenant twin does) — decide
      whether service supports those engines or the env decode narrows per kind.
  [?] An empty `RESTIC_SNAPSHOT` still builds a live `restic restore ... --target /` — decide
      whether the lane must fail fast on the empty selector or keeps riding restic's rc.

Done when:
  Every Scenario carries a passing mock-engine test against ./restore.rhai (resolver list
    including scripts/service), the frozen order and the five throw texts still match the
    source, and `specdd lint` is green on this file.
